WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.920
He has a story to tell and a phone to share it.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.770
On the other side, people want to watch.

3
00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:14.540
But how can he reach a huge audience without
sending a separate copy of the video to every
person?

4
00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:19.340
The distribution does not have to appear fully
formed.

5
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.940
When sessions of the same transmission follow
the same stretch, an aggregator can

6
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.680
cooperate with its neighbor and transfer those
duplicate sessions to it.

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.280
The next device then distributes the data to the
destinations on that branch.

8
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.900
Further along, the same cooperation can happen
again.

9
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:45.870
As routes are simplified, a neighbor transfers
duplicate sessions to the next device,

10
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.170
which takes over another branch.

11
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.390
The tree expands through these local
cooperations.

12
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:57.830
The creator keeps speaking as distribution takes
shape along the paths to the viewers.

13
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.050
The transmission has its own reference.

14
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.390
It is not the creator's identity, or the session
being used at that moment.

15
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.350
Replacing a session does not have to mean
creating another transmission or changing

16
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.260
who is authorized to send.

17
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.940
Helping distribute the stream does not grant the
right to speak on his behalf.

18
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:25.700
Aggregators can replicate protected data without
becoming the source or taking over viewer
admission.

19
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.400
Content authenticity must remain verifiable by
those receiving it.

20
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.120
No particular platform has to own this
distribution.

21
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:40.270
But paths, capacity, and participants willing to
carry the data are still needed.

22
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.580
The application handles the camera and playback.

23
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.360
The protocol organizes the communication
supporting the transmission.

24
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.450
One person can be the source.

25
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.650
Many participants can extend their reach.

26
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.710
One transmission.

27
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.770
Many viewers.

28
00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:57.310
PRP.

